This PR introduces a timer class for precise time measurements and timing operations. The implementation provides high-precision timing capabilities using CLOCK_MONOTONIC, with measurement granularity down to nanoseconds.
> Note : I made a small mistake and merged the ongoing branch of communication so that part is not up for review as it is not completely functional and well organised. This will be fixed on the next PR. 

## Features

- Named timer instances for tracking different timing contexts
- High-precision measurements in seconds, milliseconds, microseconds, and nanoseconds
- Static sleep functions for precise delays
- Application uptime tracking
- Error handling using result type
- 
## Core Functionality

### Timer Instance Methods

- `start()`: Begins timing measurement
- `stop()`: Ends timing measurement
- `reset()`: Clears timer state
- `elapsed_s()`, `elapsed_ms()`, `elapsed_us()`, `elapsed_ns()`: Get elapsed time in different units
- `is_running()`: Check timer state

### Static Utility Methods

- `sleep_ms()`, `sleep_us()`, `sleep_ns()`: Precise sleep durations
- `timestamp()`: Get current monotonic time
- `timestamp_ms()`: Get current time in milliseconds
- `apptime_s()`, `apptime_ms()`, `apptime_us()`, `apptime_ns()`: Get application uptime in different units

## Example Usage

```cpp
// Basic timer usage
timer t("operation_timer");

// Start timing
auto start_result = t.start();
if (start_result.has_error()) {
    Logger::error("Failed to start timer: {}", start_result.get_error_msg());
    return;
}

// Your operation here
timer::sleep_ms(100) // Sleep for 100ms

// Stop timing
auto stop_result = t.stop();
if (stop_result.has_error()) {
    Logger::error("Failed to stop timer: {}", stop_result.get_error_msg());
    return;
}

// Get elapsed time in different units
auto ms = t.elapsed_ms();
if (!ms.has_error()) {
    Logger::info("Operation took {} milliseconds", ms.get_value());
}

// Static utility examples
// Sleep for 50 microseconds
timer::sleep_us(50);

// Get current timestamp
auto ts = timer::timestamp();
if (!ts.has_error()) {
    Logger::info("Current timestamp: {}.{}", 
                 ts.get_value().tv_sec, 
                 ts.get_value().tv_nsec);
}

// Get application uptime
auto uptime = timer::apptime_ms();
if (!uptime.has_error()) {
    Logger::info("Application has been running for {} ms", uptime.get_value());
}
```

## Implementation Notes

- Uses CLOCK_MONOTONIC for reliable time measurements
- All operations return result<T> for proper error handling
- Timers track their running state to prevent invalid operations
- Application start time is captured automatically at program initialization

## Testing
Please refer to `/tests/utils/test_timer.cpp` for more detailed information about the test. 
The test cover the following : 
1. Basic timer functionality (start/stop/reset)
2. Error handling for invalid operations
3. Sleep precision at different granularities
4. Application uptime tracking accuracy
5. Timestamp generation reliability

## Future Improvements

- [ ] Implement thread safety for timer instances
- [ ] Refactor time_diff to return result<timespec> directly ( i used a hack to avoid rewriting a bunch of things)
- [ ] Add support for timer pause/resume
- [ ] Add benchmarking utilities built on top of the timer class
